Complaints, Vulnerable Customers & Accessibility
1. What counts as a complaint
A complaint is an expression of dissatisfaction about Aurora Bank’s products, services, staff, systems, fees, decisions, delays, or complaint handling. A customer does not need to use the word "complaint" for Aurora Bank to treat it as one.
Examples include:
- a payment delay caused by bank error;
- a fee the customer believes is wrong;
- poor branch or telephone service;
- declined card or payment where the customer believes the decision was wrong;
- disagreement with a fraud or chargeback outcome;
- accessibility needs not met;
- concerns about data privacy;
- concerns about lending affordability or collections behavior.

Complaints, Vulnerable Customers & Accessibility
2. How to make a complaint
Customers can complain through:
- secure message in online banking;
- telephone banking;
- branch appointment;
- postal letter;
- email to the complaints team where available;
- an authorized representative, attorney, guardian, or support worker;
- accessibility-assisted channel.
The customer should provide account details, contact details, what happened, relevant dates, amounts, names, evidence, and the outcome requested. Customers should not send passwords, PINs, or one-time passcodes.

Complaints, Vulnerable Customers & Accessibility
4. What Aurora Bank may do to resolve a complaint
Depending on the facts, Aurora Bank may:
- apologize;
- correct an error;
- refund a fee or interest;
- make a goodwill payment;
- update records;
- reopen an investigation;
- provide staff feedback or training;
- improve a process;
- explain why the original decision was correct;
- refer the customer to an external dispute resolution body.
Aurora Bank does not provide compensation for every inconvenience, and redress depends on evidence, legal obligations, financial loss, distress, and fairness.

Complaints, Vulnerable Customers & Accessibility
6. Vulnerable customer support
A customer may be vulnerable because of permanent, temporary, or situational circumstances. Examples include disability, serious illness, bereavement, mental health difficulty, addiction, financial abuse, domestic abuse, low literacy, language barriers, digital exclusion, unemployment, displacement, or sudden income shock.
Aurora Bank can offer support such as:
- alternative communication formats;
- extra time to provide documents;
- branch appointments in private rooms;
- third-party support with customer permission;
- trusted contact notes;
- temporary card or payment controls;
- debt-support referral;
- priority handling for urgent hardship;
- interpreter or relay services;
- simplified explanations of fees, credit, and repayment options.
Support is tailored to circumstances and product rules. Some requests may require evidence or legal authority.

Complaints, Vulnerable Customers & Accessibility
7. Accessibility adjustments
Aurora Bank can provide reasonable adjustments such as:
- large print, Braille, audio, or accessible PDF documents;
- telephone relay support;
- sign-language appointment where available;
- step-free branch appointment where possible;
- longer appointment slots;
- secure message instead of phone call;
- assistance reading forms;
- accessible card design or tactile card features where offered;
- paper statements without fee where digital access is not suitable because of disability.
Customers can request adjustments at any time. Some formats may take longer to prepare.

Complaints, Vulnerable Customers & Accessibility
9. Financial difficulty
Customers who cannot pay bills, loan repayments, overdrafts, or credit-card balances should contact Aurora Bank as early as possible. Early contact gives more options.
Support may include:
- income and expenditure review;
- temporary payment arrangement;
- overdraft review;
- breathing-space or moratorium process where available;
- referral to free debt advice;
- freezing certain charges where appropriate;
- restructuring or term extension where allowed;
- collections hold while evidence is reviewed.
Financial difficulty support may affect credit reporting depending on the arrangement.

Account Opening, Eligibility & Customer Verification Policy
1. Who can apply
Aurora Bank normally accepts applications from natural persons who meet all of the following requirements:
- are at least 18 years old for a standard personal current account or savings account;
- have legal capacity to enter into a banking relationship;
- can provide valid proof of identity and proof of residential address;
- can explain the expected purpose and use of the account;
- are not subject to legal or sanctions restrictions that prevent us from providing services;
- agree to the account terms, privacy notice, electronic communications terms, and applicable fee schedule.
Applicants aged 15 to 17 may apply for a Youth Current Account with consent from a parent or legal guardian. Youth accounts have reduced limits, no arranged overdraft, and no credit-card eligibility.
Aurora Bank may offer a Basic Payment Account to customers who meet legal eligibility criteria but may not qualify for a standard packaged account. Basic accounts provide essential payment services but do not include credit facilities.

Account Opening, Eligibility & Customer Verification Policy
2. Residency and address requirements
A customer does not always need to be a citizen of the country where the account is opened. However, Aurora Bank must be able to verify the customer’s identity, address, tax residency, and expected account activity.
We may request additional information where:
- the customer recently moved to the country;
- the customer lives in one country but receives income in another;
- the customer has more than one tax residency;
- the customer is a student, posted worker, diplomat, cross-border employee, or seasonal worker;
- the customer cannot provide a standard utility bill or lease agreement;
- the customer’s address is temporary, shared, institutional, or a correspondence-only address.
A post-office box or virtual mailbox is not accepted as the sole residential address. A correspondence address can be added after a residential address has been verified.

Account Opening, Eligibility & Customer Verification Policy
3. Documents we may request
3.2 Proof of address
We normally accept one of the following dated within the last 90 days:
- utility bill for electricity, gas, water, fixed broadband, or municipal services;
- bank statement issued by a regulated financial institution;
- lease agreement with landlord details and property address;
- official tax, social security, or municipal letter;
- employment confirmation showing a residential address, only where accompanied by another supporting document;
- university housing confirmation for students.
Mobile phone bills, handwritten letters, parcel receipts, and screenshots that do not show the issuer and date are not normally sufficient.

Account Opening, Eligibility & Customer Verification Policy
4. Application channels
Customers may apply through:
1. Aurora Online Banking or the mobile application;
2. an Aurora branch or partner branch;
3. telephone-assisted onboarding for customers who need accessibility support;
4. a workplace, student, or relocation onboarding program approved by Aurora Bank.
Online applications can usually be completed without visiting a branch. We may still require branch verification if documents are unclear, fraud indicators are present, or the customer is applying for a product with enhanced checks.

Account Opening, Eligibility & Customer Verification Policy
5. What happens during verification
Aurora Bank may carry out the following checks:
- identity document validation;
- address verification;
- sanctions and politically exposed person screening;
- fraud database and internal risk checks;
- credit reference agency checks where a credit facility is requested;
- tax residency and common reporting standard checks;
- review of adverse media or publicly available information where relevant to financial-crime obligations.
For non-credit accounts, credit bureau information is not normally used to decide whether the customer can open a basic current account, but it may be used to verify identity or assess an overdraft request.

Account Opening, Eligibility & Customer Verification Policy
7. Joint accounts
A joint account is owned by two or more customers. Each account holder must complete identity and address verification. Unless the account mandate says otherwise, any one account holder may give payment instructions, use a debit card issued in their own name, view statements, and close certain services.
Important rules for joint accounts:
- all account holders are jointly and severally responsible for fees, overdrafts, and negative balances;
- one account holder’s debt or legal restriction may affect the account;
- account holders should agree privately how money in the account is owned, because Aurora Bank does not normally decide beneficial shares;
- if there is a dispute between account holders, Aurora Bank may restrict withdrawals or require joint instructions;
- a joint account is not suitable where customers cannot trust each other to operate the account responsibly.
A joint account may be converted to a sole account only with the consent of all account holders and subject to product eligibility. If one account holder dies, the process in [Account Closure, Inactivity, Estates & Power of Attorney](11_account_closure_inactivity_estates_and_power_of_attorney.md) applies.

Account Opening, Eligibility & Customer Verification Policy
11. Reasons we may refuse an application
Aurora Bank may refuse to open an account where:
- identity cannot be verified;
- required documents are not provided;
- the customer provides false, misleading, or incomplete information;
- the account appears likely to be used for unlawful activity;
- sanctions, court orders, or regulatory restrictions apply;
- the customer has previously misused Aurora Bank services;
- the product is not suitable or available in the customer’s location;
- the customer does not agree to the required terms.
Where possible, we will give a general reason for refusal. We may not disclose detailed fraud, sanctions, or risk-screening reasons.

Account Opening, Eligibility & Customer Verification Policy
13. Account activation
An account is normally activated when:
1. the account holder’s identity is verified;
2. all required declarations are accepted;
3. the customer receives account details;
4. any initial deposit requirement is met;
5. the customer sets up secure access credentials.
Debit cards and online banking access may be issued separately. Credit cards, overdrafts, and loans require separate approval.

Current & Savings Accounts Terms
2. Current account core services
A current account normally allows the customer to:
- receive salary, pension, benefits, refunds, and other incoming payments;
- make SEPA credit transfers and, where available, instant payments;
- set up standing orders and direct debits;
- use a debit card for purchases, cash withdrawals, and online payments;
- use mobile wallet services where the device and card are eligible;
- view balances, statements, and payment confirmations;
- send secure messages to Aurora Bank;
- request account documents such as balance confirmations or interest certificates.
A current account is not a safe-deposit service, investment product, escrow arrangement, or business merchant-processing account.

Current & Savings Accounts Terms
12. Account restrictions
Aurora Bank may restrict an account where:
- security is at risk;
- suspicious or unauthorized activity is detected;
- the customer has not provided required information;
- a court order, tax order, enforcement order, sanctions requirement, or legal notice applies;
- the account is being used outside product terms;
- a dispute between joint account holders cannot be resolved;
- the customer is insolvent or subject to a legal process.
We will inform the customer where we are legally allowed to do so. Restrictions can affect cards, online banking, payments, cash withdrawals, statements, and account closure.

Privacy, Data Protection & Open Banking
3. Why we use data
Aurora Bank uses customer data to:
- verify identity and address;
- open and operate accounts;
- process payments and card transactions;
- assess credit applications and affordability;
- prevent fraud, scams, money laundering, terrorist financing, and sanctions breaches;
- comply with tax, accounting, legal, regulatory, and reporting obligations;
- provide customer support and resolve complaints;
- manage risk and improve products;
- send service messages and legal notices;
- personalize digital banking features;
- provide marketing where permitted;
- support vulnerable customers where the customer asks for help or law permits processing.

Privacy, Data Protection & Open Banking
6. Sharing data
Aurora Bank may share data with:
- payment systems and card schemes;
- beneficiary and sending banks;
- credit reference agencies;
- fraud prevention agencies;
- regulators, tax authorities, courts, law enforcement, and ombudsman bodies;
- service providers such as IT hosting, card production, statement printing, call-center systems, and document verification providers;
- debt collection agencies and legal advisers where permitted;
- insurers or benefit providers for packaged accounts;
- open banking providers where the customer authorizes access;
- persons appointed by the customer, such as attorneys, guardians, executors, or authorized representatives.
We do not sell customer transaction data to advertisers.

Privacy, Data Protection & Open Banking
9. Customer rights
Subject to legal limits, customers may have the right to:
- access their personal data;
- correct inaccurate data;
- erase data where retention is no longer required;
- restrict processing;
- object to processing based on legitimate interests;
- receive certain data in portable format;
- withdraw consent;
- object to direct marketing;
- request human review of certain automated decisions;
- complain to a data protection authority.
Some rights may be limited where data is needed for account operation, legal obligations, financial crime prevention, legal claims, or rights of others.

Security, Online Banking & Customer Responsibilities
3. What Aurora Bank will never ask for
Aurora Bank will never ask a customer to:
- disclose a full card PIN;
- disclose a full online banking password;
- reveal a one-time passcode to approve a transaction the customer did not create;
- move money to a safe account;
- ignore security warnings;
- install remote-access software because of an unsolicited call;
- give a card to a courier;
- lie to branch staff or other banks about the purpose of a payment;
- send money to receive a refund, prize, job, loan, or investment withdrawal.
A request like this should be treated as suspicious.

Security, Online Banking & Customer Responsibilities
4. Customer security duties
Customers must take reasonable steps to keep accounts safe, including:
- keeping cards, devices, PINs, passwords, passcodes, and security tokens secure;
- using unique passwords for banking;
- keeping phone numbers and email addresses up to date;
- reviewing statements and notifications;
- reporting suspicious activity promptly;
- using trusted networks and updated devices;
- logging out of shared devices;
- not sharing credentials with family, friends, merchants, or third-party apps unless the process is an approved open banking flow;
- not bypassing bank warnings or controls.
Failure to follow these duties can affect liability for unauthorized transactions.

Security, Online Banking & Customer Responsibilities
5. Phishing, smishing, and vishing
Phishing uses fake emails, websites, or messages. Smishing uses SMS or chat messages. Vishing uses phone calls. Scammers may pretend to be Aurora Bank, police, tax authorities, delivery companies, investment platforms, employers, or family members.
Customers should check:
- whether the message creates urgency or fear;
- whether the link domain is genuine;
- whether the caller asks for credentials or payment approval;
- whether the request matches normal bank behavior;
- whether contact details came from a known official source.
If uncertain, customers should stop the interaction and contact Aurora Bank using a known channel from [Glossary, Contact Channels & Service Levels](12_glossary_contact_and_service_level.md).

Security, Online Banking & Customer Responsibilities
8. Device security
Customers should keep devices secure by:
- using screen lock and biometric protection;
- installing operating system and app updates;
- using anti-malware tools where appropriate;
- avoiding jailbroken or rooted devices for banking;
- downloading apps only from official stores;
- revoking access for lost or old devices;
- not approving login prompts they did not initiate.
Aurora Bank may block access from devices that appear compromised.
